<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.11.1: Relating a Quadratic Function and Its Zeros</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02265</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.11.1: Relating a Quadratic Function and Its Zeros</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4113dbbe-b1a3-4128-ba16-c8afce5f9571</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>This warm up refreshes students’ ability to convert a quadratic function from standard form to factored form. It also provides practice deriving the solutions, or zeros, of a function from its factored form. </para>
<para>Students will use graphing technology to analyze the graph of a quadratic function by finding the \(x\)-intercepts and zeros.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of two. Ask students to work through the questions individually, but ask their partner if they have a question. </para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the quadratic function \(f(x) = x^2 − 9x + 20\) in factored form.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x) = (x − 4)(x − 5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the solutions of the quadratic equation \((x − 4)(x − 5) = 0\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x = 4\) and \(x = 5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Graph the quadratic function \(f(x) = x^2− 9x + 20\). </item>
</list>
<para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. (Students were provided access to Desmos.)</para>
<para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph?</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((4, 0)\), \((5, 0)\) or 4 and 5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Marcus states that the zeros of the function of \(f(x) = x^2 − 9x + 20\) are 4 and 5. Do you agree? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes, 4 and 5 are the input values that result in an output of 0. When the function \(f(x) = x^2 − 9x + 20\) is graphed, it crosses the \(x\)-axis at \(x=4\) and \(x=5\).</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ensure students understand moving from standard form to factored form to deriving solutions or zeros using a quadratic function. After students work through the questions, ask the class as a whole if there are any outstanding questions.</para>
<para>These are important skills that this lesson builds on as students learn more about writing a quadratic function when given its zeros.</para></section>
</content>
